From e8bd04259d43276c4a8cf6ac7c6ab8758afdca8a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 02:39:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Implement host-authoritative LAN multiplayer

Players who joined a table could not obtain a character sheet: the client
branch of handleLoadCharacter was a console.log stub, and every sheet
operation invoked Tauri with a local filesystem path that a joined client
does not have.

The host is now authoritative. Clients address characters by sheet id and
call the host over the existing WebSocket; the host runs the same api
functions its own window runs and announces every write.

Backend
- api.rs: rules and persistence extracted from the Tauri command handlers so
  IPC and LAN share one implementation. commands.rs is now a thin adapter.
- network/: wire protocol, Axum server lifecycle, per-connection RPC dispatch.
- state.rs, campaign.rs, history.rs: shared state, portable game-instance
  provisioning, SQLite session log for reconnects.

Fixes
- create_game_instance resolved templates from a hardcoded developer home
  directory; it now uses Tauri resource/data dirs and campaigns are bundled.
- The LAN server bound a port on every instance at startup, before any game
  existed; it now starts on host and stops on leave.
- Players were never removed from the roster on disconnect and the socket
  task leaked once recv() returned None.
- Secret rolls were broadcast to every client and only hidden in the UI.
- The character list was hardcoded, so clients could not discover the party.
- Sheet ids are resolved inside the game root and writes are permission
  checked, so a client cannot read or edit arbitrary paths.
- Added the missing tsconfig.json; tsc -b (and so tauri build) failed without it.
